## Context

Agent-driven engineering has matured to where the bottleneck is no longer "can the agent write code" but "does the agent know what the code is supposed to do." Pattern-driven approaches — copy a template, mimic a previous module, follow the codebase's style — produce locally plausible work that drifts from the team's actual intent. The agent has no durable record of *why* a module exists; only of how its neighbors look.

AIDE addresses that gap by making intent the first-class artifact. A short spec lives next to the code it governs, captures purpose and falsifiable success criteria, and becomes the contract every downstream agent works from. When intent changes, code changes; when code drifts from intent, the code is wrong.

A methodology like that only delivers on its promise if every team using it follows the same doctrine. A team that paraphrases the rules into their own house guide is practicing a fork, not the methodology. Two adopters claiming compliance behave differently and neither can detect why. AIDE therefore exists as a **product**: adopters receive the methodology fully formed, they do not author it. The doctrine, the spec format, the agent roles, and the playbook structure ship as one coherent thing that evolves centrally.

That posture creates obligations. The product must keep its cost proportional to use, must not lock adopters in, and must produce artifacts that survive on their own engineering merits if a team walks away.

## Strategy

**Methodology ships as a product, not as published guidance.** A rule that lives only in a public document gets paraphrased — adopters retype it, lift the parts they like, and over months the local copy drifts. A methodology delivered as a product is consumed as-is: the doctrine, the agent roles, the spec format, and the playbook structure arrive together and stay aligned with the canonical authority. Adopters cite the source they use; reviewers settle disputes by pointing at one place.

**Intent is the durable artifact; code is its current expression.** Tests, prompts, and code all rewrite under the same intent across a module's lifetime. AIDE puts the spec — purpose, falsifiable outcomes, failure modes that pass tests but violate intent — at the center of every agent's context, and treats implementation as ephemeral. This is what lets an agent answer "is this output correct?" rather than only "does it compile?", and what lets a refactor preserve meaning instead of just behavior.

**The intent tree cascades; children narrow, never restate.** Specs form a hierarchy from the project root down to the deepest module, and a child spec inherits everything above it. Agents traverse root to leaf to assemble full context for any module, and a child only carries what is specific to its slice. This keeps each spec lean, makes the locus of every decision discoverable, and prevents the parent-level mission from being copy-pasted across siblings where it would drift independently.

**Authoring is centralized; evolution is one-way.** Decentralized authoring forks: every adopter customizing their copy guarantees that within a year "AIDE" no longer means one thing across teams. AIDE accepts the constraint of a single canonical authority so improvements reach every adopter coherently, contradictions get settled in one place, and two teams describing the methodology describe the same thing. Adopters extend AIDE by writing their own specs and their own playbook entries — not by editing AIDE.

**Reversibility is a non-negotiable adoption term.** A methodology that locks adopters into a particular agent runtime, IDE, vendor, or knowledge backend fails the ethics-of-tools test and becomes uncomfortable to recommend. AIDE's specs are written as engineering documents first — purpose, success criteria, failure modes in plain language — so a team walking away from the methodology walks away with artifacts that still document what their system was built to do. The intent tree survives the tool that produced it.

**Cost stays proportional to AIDE-touching work.** A methodology that taxes every session regardless of task is a methodology adopters stop reading. AIDE keeps the doctrine off the per-session context budget for tasks that don't touch it, and pulls in only the slice of the methodology a given task actually needs. A team using AIDE on part of their codebase pays nothing on the rest; a session whose task has nothing to do with AIDE doesn't carry the methodology's full weight.

## Good examples

**A team adopts AIDE and gets coherent doctrine without authoring it.** A four-engineer team starts using AIDE on one new feature. They never write the spec format, the agent roles, or the playbook structure themselves — those arrive as part of the methodology. When a junior engineer asks "what does AIDE say about outcome lists?", the answer is one canonical document, not a folklore reconstruction. Six months later they describe AIDE the same way another team using it describes AIDE.

**A maintainer improvement reaches every adopter coherently.** The methodology gets a sharper rule about what belongs in outcomes versus what belongs in code. Adopters who refresh their installation see the same improvement, framed the same way, with the same examples. No team has to chase a blog post update or merge a PR into their private copy.

**A team leaves AIDE and the specs still read as engineering documents.** Eighteen months in, a team decides AIDE isn't right for them. They stop using the agent pipeline. The specs they wrote still describe — in plain language — what each module is for and what it must not do. The intent tree functions as architectural documentation independent of the methodology that produced it.

## Bad examples

**A rule gets paraphrased into an adopter's house guide and silently drifts.** A team copies the AIDE outcomes guidance into their internal CLAUDE.md, lightly reworded. A year later the canonical guidance has been sharpened twice; the local copy has not. The team believes they are following AIDE; their behavior diverges from other adopters in ways no review catches because the discrepancy lives in tone, not in any single rule.

**Doctrine taxes every session and adopters quietly stop loading it.** A team configures their agent harness to load the full methodology on every chat regardless of task. Token budgets balloon, agents slow down, sessions get expensive. Within weeks the team has truncated the load to "the parts we actually use" — meaning a private fork has appeared inside their config. The methodology that preached proportional cost has itself become the thing every session must carry.

**AIDE forks and "AIDE" stops meaning one thing.** Two adopting teams customize their copies of the methodology in different directions. A new hire moving between teams encounters two different rule sets, both called AIDE, neither labeled as a fork. Conversations about "what AIDE says" become unresolvable because there is no longer a single canonical authority to point at.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` (frontmatter) — the business outcomes every strategy decision in this body must serve
- `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` — the Brevity Contract, the forbidden-content list, and the domain-vocabulary boundary that define what may and may not appear in this body
- `cold-outreach-analyzer/src/service/outreach/sequence/.aide` — calibration reference for body shape, decision-form Strategy paragraphs, and the brevity of Good/Bad example pairs
